{
  "configuration_id": "config_006",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_002 (singleprofile, pca_n2000, no aggregation) scored 0.485 acc, while config_021 (multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) scored 0.591 acc—a +0.106 jump showing that multi-profile aggregation is highly beneficial. However, we only know avgpool works; we don't know whether a learned transformer aggregation captures inter-profile interactions that simple averaging misses. Question: config_006 is a clean single-variable change from config_021 (only aggregation: avgpool → transformer), isolating the effect of aggregation sophistication while holding encoder (pca_n2000), profile setting (multiprofile), and modality (rna_only) constant. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It directly extends our strongest result with the minimal change, giving the clearest signal. (2) The aggregation choice is a foundational architectural decision that will inform every subsequent multimodal experiment—knowing whether transformer or avgpool is superior before adding image modality prevents confounding. (3) It is computationally cheaper than testing a new learned encoder. (4) With only 2 completed experiments, reducing uncertainty on this core design axis is more valuable than jumping to a multimodal configuration where multiple new variables (image encoder, fusion, cross-modal interaction) would be introduced simultaneously."
}

